  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, an elliptic cone centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is an elliptic cone whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis and $a=1,\\,b=c=2.$",
